  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nThe amplifying upper level pattern late week suggests a Pacific\ntrough will develop off the West Coast by around Thursday with a\nsmall majority of latest runs' ensemble members forming a closed \nlow just off the coast of southern/central California by Friday. \nOther members have more of a faster and less amplified \ndisturbance which could still occur, but is a bit less likely to \nhappen. For now with the majority of the members, including the \ndeterministic members, calling for a closed low to approach the \nregion on Friday, higher level clouds will be the first feature to\nmove in from the southwest.\n\nThe latest model trends however lean more toward the low tracking\nacross northern Baja into Sonora Mexico Friday night and \nSaturday. If this track holds, we are not likely to get much lower\nlevel moisture to advect northward, along with the bulk of the \nsystem energy missing us to the south. The latest NBM/WPC PoPs \nhave been scaled back due to the potential more southerly track \nleaving mostly 10-20% chances over the lower deserts and up to 30%\nover Arizona higher terrain areas Friday night-Saturday night. \nAny further weakening of the system or farther track to the south \nwould decrease rain chances. Despite the uncertainty in rain \nchances, there is higher confidence in a cooler air mass moving \ninto our region for next weekend. Current NBM forecast \ntemperatures show highs dropping into the mid to upper 70s\nstarting Saturday. Even if this first system ends up not \nproviding any meaningful rainfall, guidance is hinting at one or \neven two additional weather systems for next week.",
